251
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:34,150
(Today's table theme: Travel)

252
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:35,350
Overall today...

253
00:08:35,420 --> 00:08:36,789
- Everyone is dressed for travel. - Looks like it.

254
00:08:36,790 --> 00:08:39,260
- We were told to dress for a trip. - But don't you think...

255
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:40,720
yours does not really fit the vibe?

256
00:08:41,060 --> 00:08:42,589
- Come on, this is a travel outfit. - All right,

257
00:08:42,590 --> 00:08:43,630
show us the look.

258
00:08:43,690 --> 00:08:45,206
You look like someone who makes pottery...

259
00:08:45,230 --> 00:08:46,500
in Icheon, Gyeonggi Province.

260
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:49,800
Whenever people go on cruises, they always dress like this.

261
00:08:49,970 --> 00:08:51,700
- Older folks do. - Oh, a cruise look.

262
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:55,210
It definitely gives cruise vibes. Just keeping it simple.

263
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:57,510
I went with a knit top.

264
00:08:58,040 --> 00:08:59,340
Wow, Jaemin.

265
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:00,410
A knit top.

266
00:09:00,411 --> 00:09:02,449
(What's this situation?)

267
00:09:02,450 --> 00:09:03,756
- Wow, Jaemin. - With a tank top underneath.

268
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:05,980
This knit gives off resort vibes.

269
00:09:06,050 --> 00:09:07,819
Mine is kind of a casual beach look too.

270
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:09,190
Right.

271
00:09:10,390 --> 00:09:11,660
Mine is a lovely style.

272
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,260
Very Hawaii vibes.

273
00:09:15,290 --> 00:09:17,190
Jae Hwan, what is the vibe of your outfit?

274
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:20,460
Mine is kind of a preppy scout look, like I am going on a school trip.

275
00:09:20,870 --> 00:09:22,830
- With a shirt and tie. - Preppy scout?

276
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:24,316
- So detailed. Nice. - You wore a tie?

277
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:26,740
BamBam, are you going for a vintage style today?

278
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:30,739
- Like a stylish guy from the 1960s. - Can we see how your pants fit?

279
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:34,610
Well, they're nothing special. No, wait. Why?

280
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:36,880
I have seen this outfit in LA, though.

281
00:09:36,950 --> 00:09:39,920
- You have seen this outfit? - If you put on...

282
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:40,990
some roller skates...

283
00:09:41,250 --> 00:09:42,990
Do you know Queen? Holding a mic like this.

284
00:09:43,050 --> 00:09:45,960
- "Bohemian Rhapsody." - "Mama." Yes, exactly.

285
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:48,860
I went for a youthful hip-hop look.

286
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:52,030
Look at Yu, he looks like a cloud today.

287
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:55,030
Kind of like a handsome guy making cocktails...

288
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:58,870
- at a beach bar, you know? - Wait, back in LA...

289
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:00,616
- LA again? - I saw a guy like him at a park.

290
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:02,540
- Where, where? - At a theme park in LA.

291
00:10:02,710 --> 00:10:05,880
It feels like Jae Hwan experienced every piece of new culture in LA.

292
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:08,080
Yes, LA is all I can think about.

293
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:09,880
LA must have impressed you.

294
00:10:09,950 --> 00:10:11,950
- It left a strong impression. - That must be why.

295
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:15,050
I bet Jae Hwan ate some amazing food in LA.

296
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:16,360
Have you tried In-N-Out Burger?

297
00:10:16,390 --> 00:10:17,759
- It was delicious. - Was it good?

298
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:20,560
I literally jogged there from my hotel to eat it.

299
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:22,760
(Gosh)

300
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:23,999
(Running the sunny streets of LA)

301
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:25,260
(Sunscreen guy)

302
00:10:25,330 --> 00:10:26,959
(Sunscreen guy's LA burger mukbang)

303
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:28,069
(This is the taste of LA.)

304
00:10:28,070 --> 00:10:29,550
And the pizza on Hollywood Boulevard.

305
00:10:29,870 --> 00:10:32,039
- The pizza was so good, - The pizza?

306
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:33,216
And it was kind of a dream of mine.

307
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:35,569
- Standing, eating a pizza slice... - Eating on your feet?

308
00:10:35,570 --> 00:10:37,540
- And the size was huge. - Right.

309
00:10:37,640 --> 00:10:39,400
Did you learn how to eat cilantro in LA too?

310
00:10:39,540 --> 00:10:42,340
- I've learned everything from LA. - Everything from LA. That is nice.

311
00:10:43,150 --> 00:10:44,296
Minnie, do you have any memorable food...

312
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:48,120
- from your travels? - I recently went to Busan...

313
00:10:48,190 --> 00:10:50,019
- with my parents. - To Busan?

314
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:51,560
That famous daegutang? The cod soup?

315
00:10:51,660 --> 00:10:52,919
- Cod soup? - Cod soup.

316
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:54,590
I tried cod soup for the first time.

317
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:57,959
- For the first time? - My parents loved it,

318
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:00,059
- but my brother and I thought... - A little?

319
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:03,270
A little bland, you know? Yes.

320
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:06,239
Because the cod soup isn't seasoned that heavily...

321
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:08,070
- Yes. - and they just keep it clear.

322
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:09,180
So it's a clear soup dish?

323
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:11,980
- I haven't tried it yet. - But the fish was really...

324
00:11:12,040 --> 00:11:15,350
nice, kind of chewy?

325
00:11:15,650 --> 00:11:16,930
Wait, why eat cod soup in Busan?

326
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:18,126
Shouldn't you eat it in Daegu?

327
00:11:18,150 --> 00:11:19,420
It's famous...

328
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,719
Cod soup is famous throughout the South Gyeongsang Province anyway.

329
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:23,789
Wait, BamBam thinks if it's cod soup,

330
00:11:23,790 --> 00:11:24,859
you have to go to Daegu to eat it.

331
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:26,159
- Oh, right. - I see.

332
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:29,630
- It's not from Daegu? - No, the fish is called cod.

333
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:32,560
Cod. Oh, kind of like gamjatang.

334
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:33,900
I got it.

335
00:11:34,900 --> 00:11:35,900
It's pork backbone.

336
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:39,900
- It's cod. - I got it.

337
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,039
(He understood the meaning of it perfectly.)

338
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:43,040
Oh, he thought it meant the city.

339
00:11:43,041 --> 00:11:45,639
(Now, they learn more about each other's culture.)

340
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:47,610
How about our IDOL'S TABLE family members...

341
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,056
put together a one-day food tour...

342
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:51,420
for three meals a day, showing what to eat...

343
00:11:51,850 --> 00:11:54,389
if we traveled to your hometowns?

344
00:11:54,390 --> 00:11:55,390
I love it.

345
00:11:55,391 --> 00:11:57,520
If it were me, for breakfast,

346
00:11:57,590 --> 00:11:58,636
I'd first go with youtiao...

347
00:11:58,660 --> 00:12:02,360
- I really want to try that. - or tofu pudding.

348
00:12:02,430 --> 00:12:04,960
And for lunch, I recommend a home-cooked meal.

349
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:06,106
- Home-cooked? - Home-cooked meal?

350
00:12:06,130 --> 00:12:08,250
How can we eat a home-cooked meal with no home there?

351
00:12:08,370 --> 00:12:10,206
You can eat a home-cooked meal because you have a home.

352
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:11,900
We don't have a home in Shanghai.

353
00:12:11,970 --> 00:12:13,369
I'll invite you over.

354
00:12:13,370 --> 00:12:15,369
(The homecoming day to Lele's house is about to come.)

355
00:12:15,370 --> 00:12:17,756
Otherwise, I think going to eat traditional Shanghai food...

356
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,080
is the way to go.

357
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:21,049
- Like Dongpo pork. - Dongpo pork.

358
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:22,550
Dongpo pork is delicious, and...

359
00:12:22,610 --> 00:12:24,820
for dinner, I recommend Guangdong food.

360
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:27,320
So there's dim sum, and there's also...

361
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:29,849
my absolute favorite, shrimp rice soup.

362
00:12:29,850 --> 00:12:30,890
Shrimp soup?

363
00:12:30,960 --> 00:12:33,020
They make broth with shrimp and tomatoes,

364
00:12:33,090 --> 00:12:34,660
and add puffed rice into it.

365
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:35,889
It's so good.

366
00:12:35,890 --> 00:12:38,630
(Chenle's one-day food tour: China edition)

367
00:12:38,730 --> 00:12:39,799
Thailand.

368
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:42,229
Yes, we eat a lot of congee for breakfast.

369
00:12:42,230 --> 00:12:43,269
Congee?

370
00:12:43,270 --> 00:12:47,440
There's a dish called jok moo. They add minced pork meatballs...

371
00:12:47,510 --> 00:12:49,339
on white congee.

372
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:50,440
(Thai congee: Jok moo)

373
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:52,410
The key is patongo.

374
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:53,710
- Oh, patongo. - Right.

375
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:55,626
It looks kind of like a butterfly, on both sides...

376
00:12:55,650 --> 00:12:56,810
It looks like this.

377
00:12:56,880 --> 00:12:59,049
Does it look like a Thai twisted donut?

378
00:12:59,050 --> 00:13:02,090
- Yes, yes. It looks like that. - A Thai twisted donut.

379
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:03,526
(Just like BamBam said, butterfly-shaped dough)

380
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:06,560
You drink warm soy milk with it. It's kind of a set, yes.

381
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:08,436
- That's basically the classic way. - Right?

382
00:13:08,460 --> 00:13:09,736
Totally, it's the absolute classic.

383
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:11,906
After a hearty breakfast, what should we have for lunch?

384
00:13:11,930 --> 00:13:13,160
A hearty lunch too.

385
00:13:14,770 --> 00:13:16,600
- Always. Always hearty. - Always hearty.

386
00:13:16,670 --> 00:13:18,440
Have you ever heard of boat noodles?

387
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:23,040
It's like rice noodles, but the soup is brown.

388
00:13:23,110 --> 00:13:25,110
The key is that they add blood.

389
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:27,579
Not blood curd, but before adding the soup,

390
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:30,249
- they add the blood first. - Uncoagulated?

391
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:31,880
It's uncoagulated, real blood.

392
00:13:31,950 --> 00:13:33,149
When you pour the hot broth into it,

393
00:13:33,150 --> 00:13:36,890
the blood immediately cooks, so it spreads out like egg ribbons.

394
00:13:36,950 --> 00:13:38,190
Like this.

395
00:13:38,260 --> 00:13:39,290
Isn't it gamey?

396
00:13:39,460 --> 00:13:41,160
Not at all, it's definitely not gamey.

397
00:13:41,230 --> 00:13:42,760
It's really so delicious.

398
00:13:42,830 --> 00:13:45,230
For dinner, I recommend food just like a home-cooked meal.

399
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:46,806
What if you don't have a home?

400
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:48,346
Talking about a home-cooked food again.

401
00:13:48,370 --> 00:13:51,040
I don't have a home in Thailand.

402
00:13:51,440 --> 00:13:52,700
Wherever you go,

403
00:13:52,770 --> 00:13:55,370
- home cooking has a distinct charm. - Of course it does.

404
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:56,510
Like homestyle dishes.

405
00:13:56,570 --> 00:13:58,979
Homestyle, exactly. Like stir-fried morning glory...

406
00:13:58,980 --> 00:14:01,150
- I love that. - Or crab curry.

407
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:03,350
(Foolproof rice stealers, crab curry)

408
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:05,050
So good, so good.

409
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:06,720
You can eat so many different things.

410
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:08,390
Like tom yum goong.

411
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:10,566
Now that we're talking about Thai food, I think like,

412
00:14:10,590 --> 00:14:13,490
- "I'm having Thai food tonight." - Me too, me too.

413
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:16,590
(Minnie and BamBam's one-day food tour: Thailand edition)

414
00:14:16,660 --> 00:14:18,100
Shall we take a trip to Japan then?

415
00:14:18,200 --> 00:14:19,630
- Sounds great. - In Japan,

416
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:20,776
convenience store food is so good.

417
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:22,970
- I always go there first. - I agree. Totally agree.

418
00:14:23,030 --> 00:14:24,739
- Totally agree. - Totally agree.

419
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:27,970
- There are onigiri and pastries, - Onigiri.

420
00:14:28,070 --> 00:14:29,940
And there are so many kinds of drinks.

421
00:14:30,010 --> 00:14:32,540
You should go to a convenience store and buy breakfast there.

422
00:14:32,610 --> 00:14:34,680
For lunch, tonkotsu ramen, of course.

423
00:14:34,750 --> 00:14:36,550
Right, it should be ramen.

424
00:14:36,810 --> 00:14:38,370
You know the famous franchise of ramen?

425
00:14:38,920 --> 00:14:40,249
(The dangerous food talk, violating the broadcasting regulations)

426
00:14:40,250 --> 00:14:42,920
For dinner, motsunabe.

427
00:14:42,990 --> 00:14:44,989
So tonight it's tripe?

428
00:14:44,990 --> 00:14:46,590
Hold on. It keeps changing.

429
00:14:47,390 --> 00:14:50,629
(Yu and Tomoya's one-day food tour: Japan edition)

430
00:14:50,630 --> 00:14:53,330
When I think of Korea,

431
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:55,430
the foods that come to mind are... For breakfast,

432
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:57,369
- first off, gukbap... - Gukbap for breakfast.

433
00:14:57,370 --> 00:15:00,400
Yes. And for lunch, spicy stir-fried pork.

434
00:15:00,470 --> 00:15:02,470
- Yes. - And then for dinner,

435
00:15:02,570 --> 00:15:04,840
grilled beef tripe to wrap it up.

436
00:15:04,910 --> 00:15:06,086
Even with gukbap for breakfast,

437
00:15:06,110 --> 00:15:07,230
there are so many varieties.

438
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,150
Personally, I'd go with pork gukbap.

439
00:15:09,210 --> 00:15:10,550
Pork gukbap in the morning?

440
00:15:10,680 --> 00:15:11,680
Isn't that perfect?

441
00:15:12,450 --> 00:15:14,220
Pork gukbap in the morning is pretty tough.

442
00:15:14,290 --> 00:15:15,626
- Man, that's a tough one. - That's a little bit heavy.

443
00:15:15,650 --> 00:15:16,726
- Why? - What's wrong with it? Too heavy?

444
00:15:16,750 --> 00:15:17,796
- No, I can't swallow... - Pork gukbap...

445
00:15:17,820 --> 00:15:20,259
- pork gukbap right after I wake up. - The pork gukbap is the best.

446
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:22,660
Jaemin, why don't you give us a recommendation...

447
00:15:22,730 --> 00:15:24,160
on a bit lighter side?

448
00:15:24,190 --> 00:15:25,800
For breakfast, keto gimbap.

449
00:15:26,430 --> 00:15:28,900
- That really is light. - Around lunchtime,

450
00:15:28,970 --> 00:15:31,170
- you've got to have some meat. - How would you eat it?

451
00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:32,470
Just grill up some pork.

452
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:33,569
Grilled pork belly?

453
00:15:33,570 --> 00:15:34,686
- Starting from lunch? - And then...

454
00:15:34,710 --> 00:15:37,110
- Grilling pork for lunch. - That's not fresh at all.

455
00:15:37,170 --> 00:15:38,456
That's why I had keto gimbap for breakfast.

456
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:40,479
- To go easy on my stomach first. - To soothe it?

457
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,310
- What about dinner? - Dinner?

458
00:15:42,610 --> 00:15:44,380
- Braised hairtail? - Braised hairtail.

459
00:15:44,980 --> 00:15:46,656
Wait, I thought you had a delicate stomach.

460
00:15:46,680 --> 00:15:47,820
(Let's just eat.)

461
00:15:47,890 --> 00:15:49,649
(Jaemin's one-day food tour of Korea: Fresh ver.)

462
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:51,436
(Jae Hwan's one-day food tour of Korea: Heavy ver.)

463
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,360
Anyway, since you guys travel all over the country...

464
00:15:54,430 --> 00:15:56,989
for promotions and schedules,

465
00:15:56,990 --> 00:16:00,360
do you have any go-to restaurants when you visit Jeju Island, Chenle?

466
00:16:00,430 --> 00:16:02,730
I really like grilled black pork,

467
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,576
and there's braised raw anchovy served with it.

468
00:16:04,600 --> 00:16:05,600
Braised raw anchovy?

469
00:16:05,670 --> 00:16:08,070
You dip the grilled pork into the sauce...

470
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:09,970
and grill it just a bit more on the griddle.

471
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,740
- I'm starving. - It's seriously so good.

472
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:16,110
I once did a whole tour of the markets in Busan.

473
00:16:16,180 --> 00:16:17,880
- Jagalchi Market? - Yes.

474
00:16:17,950 --> 00:16:19,849
Seed hotteok, spicy glass noodles...

475
00:16:19,850 --> 00:16:21,566
And the tteokbokki was surprisingly amazing.

476
00:16:21,590 --> 00:16:22,590
"Red Tteokbokki?"

477
00:16:22,790 --> 00:16:26,359
- Yes, that one. - It's named "Red Tteokbokki..."

478
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,759
Honestly, I used to think...

479
00:16:27,760 --> 00:16:28,806
tteokbokki was pretty much the same everywhere,

480
00:16:28,830 --> 00:16:30,430
- but it was incredible. - It really is.

481
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:33,599
I went to Goseong in Gangwon Province recently,

482
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:36,530
and they braise whole flounder for a long time.

483
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:39,740
So I tried that braised flounder, and it was out of this world.

484
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:41,669
- The fish bones just... - It must be good.

485
00:16:41,670 --> 00:16:43,369
Melted away in your mouth.

486
00:16:43,370 --> 00:16:45,186
- I was drooling the whole time. - I really want to try that.

487
00:16:45,210 --> 00:16:46,239
- My mouth is watering. - Does anyone here...

488
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:47,580
like stuffed squid?

489
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:48,680
- I do. - Me.

490
00:16:48,750 --> 00:16:49,786
- I love it too. - For stuffed squid,

491
00:16:49,810 --> 00:16:52,450
a whole squid is filled with minced meat...

492
00:16:52,520 --> 00:16:54,720
and glass noodles, then steamed thoroughly.

493
00:16:54,790 --> 00:16:56,220
You will definitely love it.

494
00:16:56,290 --> 00:16:57,349
It is so good.

495
00:16:57,350 --> 00:16:59,460
Any restaurant recommendations in Jeonju?

496
00:16:59,520 --> 00:17:01,460
- Bean sprout gukbap. - Gukbap.

497
00:17:01,530 --> 00:17:03,589
- Yes, bean sprout gukbap. - Bean sprout gukbap.

498
00:17:03,590 --> 00:17:05,760
Right. But when I eat bean sprout gukbap,

499
00:17:05,830 --> 00:17:07,136
I don't like putting an egg into it.

500
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:08,870
- Egg on the side. - Me too.

501
00:17:09,070 --> 00:17:10,576
Yes, I would rather just eat the poached egg...

502
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:12,000
by itself with a spoon.

503
00:17:12,070 --> 00:17:15,470
Because once it goes into the broth, it ruins the clean flavor.

504
00:17:15,870 --> 00:17:16,870
You have firm standards.

505
00:17:16,970 --> 00:17:18,780
There are people who crack the egg into it,

506
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:21,110
- and people who eat it separately. - Right.

507
00:17:21,250 --> 00:17:23,710
- Right, right. - I'm so hungry.

508
00:17:23,780 --> 00:17:25,260
My stomach has been growling nonstop.

509
00:17:26,120 --> 00:17:27,420
I know, right?

510
00:17:27,990 --> 00:17:29,589
And just as much as the food,

511
00:17:29,590 --> 00:17:32,860
knowing each country's dining culture is very important.

512
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:35,590
Things that might be rude. Table manners, for instance.

513
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:37,690
In our country...

514
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:39,660
Sticking your spoon upright in rice is a no-no.

515
00:17:39,730 --> 00:17:42,170
Right. Sticking your spoon straight into a bowl of rice is...

516
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:44,030
Exactly. And when you go to China,

517
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:45,940
- there is no cold water. - Hardly ever.

518
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,640
It is always lukewarm, warm, or even boiling hot water.

519
00:17:49,710 --> 00:17:51,609
So when I first came to Korea,

520
00:17:51,610 --> 00:17:54,550
I went to get some lukewarm water,

521
00:17:54,610 --> 00:17:56,610
and the other members were crunching on ice cubes.

522
00:17:57,050 --> 00:17:58,120
(Laughing)

523
00:17:58,620 --> 00:18:01,689
I thought, "Don't your teeth hurt?" That was a real culture shock.

524
00:18:01,690 --> 00:18:04,149
(A glass of water made him feel an unexpected culture shock.)

525
00:18:04,150 --> 00:18:06,390
What about Thailand? Any useful tips we should know?

526
00:18:06,590 --> 00:18:08,930
Well, night markets in Thailand are quite famous.

527
00:18:08,990 --> 00:18:10,759
Whenever you go to a night market...

528
00:18:10,760 --> 00:18:16,769
Many foreigners might not realize that cards are rarely accepted,

529
00:18:16,770 --> 00:18:20,640
- so you definitely need cash. - You must carry cash. That's true.

530
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:22,710
- That's such a solid tip. - Totally.

531
00:18:22,770 --> 00:18:25,479
I'll share how I find good restaurants.

532
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:27,756
- Your way of finding them? - How I find good restaurants.

533
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:29,910
The filthier...

534
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:32,580
(Already picturing that great foodie vibe)

535
00:18:32,650 --> 00:18:35,090
- You walk into the dirtiest alley, - Into a dirty alley?

536
00:18:35,190 --> 00:18:36,966
- and find the most sweltering... - Sweltering.

537
00:18:36,990 --> 00:18:39,090
And if the lady running it is rude,

538
00:18:39,160 --> 00:18:41,000
it means she is that confident in her cooking.

539
00:18:41,190 --> 00:18:43,430
Right. That's the same as here.

540
00:18:43,490 --> 00:18:46,559
Like in Korea, where an old place run by a foul-mouthed grandma...

541
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:48,299
- always means it's delicious. - Right. Same for us.

542
00:18:48,300 --> 00:18:51,900
There's a big picture of a grandma right on the sign,

543
00:18:51,970 --> 00:18:53,940
and next to it, it always says "Original."

544
00:18:54,170 --> 00:18:55,686
You have to be careful with "original," though.

545
00:18:55,710 --> 00:18:56,940
There are too many originals.

546
00:18:57,570 --> 00:18:58,610
Way too many.

547
00:18:58,680 --> 00:18:59,980
- Right. - So true, so true.

548
00:19:00,210 --> 00:19:02,749
Yu, do you have any tips for traveling in Fukuoka?

549
00:19:02,750 --> 00:19:04,380
Well, when you go to an izakaya,

550
00:19:04,450 --> 00:19:06,950
instead of looking at the printed menu,

551
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:09,120
look at the ones hanging on the wall.

552
00:19:09,190 --> 00:19:13,060
- The daily specials. - Right, the chef's specials.

553
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:14,989
Those are guaranteed to be good...

554
00:19:14,990 --> 00:19:18,900
because they use ingredients that come in that day.

555
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:22,030
- They're always delicious. - That makes sense. It's fresh.

556
00:19:22,570 --> 00:19:25,870
And before you even order your food, they bring out a little appetizer.

557
00:19:26,070 --> 00:19:27,440
It's called otoshi,

558
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:29,170
and it's kind of like a cover charge.

559
00:19:29,240 --> 00:19:30,880
So you get charged just for sitting down?

560
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:34,380
- It's around 500 yen or so. - Really? I didn't know that.

561
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:35,450
That's news to me.

562
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:37,349
You know that drawer on the side of the table?

563
00:19:37,350 --> 00:19:38,510
Foreigners can never find it.

564
00:19:39,380 --> 00:19:40,420
They can never find it.

565
00:19:40,480 --> 00:19:41,519
(Everyone who's been there bursts out laughing.)

566
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:43,789
- Oh, a drawer. - Yes, the drawer.

567
00:19:43,790 --> 00:19:45,559
- Exactly, exactly. - So true, so true.

568
00:19:45,560 --> 00:19:48,160
Don't think they forgot utensils. Just look around.

569
00:19:48,230 --> 00:19:50,229
Whenever I can't find them, I do this.

570
00:19:50,230 --> 00:19:51,299
(Fully localized. BamBam's skill for finding Korean utensils)

571
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:54,630
- That's it, that's it. - Good tip.

572
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:57,046
I really think it's adorable when foreigners go into restaurants...

573
00:19:57,070 --> 00:20:00,140
and call the staff "Boss" or "Auntie."

574
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:02,169
What's fascinating is that in Korea,

575
00:20:02,170 --> 00:20:03,309
people call...

576
00:20:03,310 --> 00:20:04,656
- every staff member "Boss." - Right.

577
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:06,410
Even part-time workers get called "Boss."

578
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:08,479
Isn't that disrespectful to the actual boss?

579
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:10,779
(Power F Tomoya's polite curiosity)

580
00:20:10,780 --> 00:20:12,096
- It could come off as rude. - Really?

581
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:13,440
To the real boss, it's kind of...

582
00:20:14,350 --> 00:20:16,689
We should address people by their rank.

583
00:20:16,690 --> 00:20:17,820
"Manager."

584
00:20:18,090 --> 00:20:20,190
- It's interesting. - "Director, Team Leader."

585
00:20:20,260 --> 00:20:21,760
Right, totally.

586
00:20:22,790 --> 00:20:23,929
All right,

587
00:20:23,930 --> 00:20:27,730
today's special dish fits perfectly with the theme of travel.

588
00:20:28,370 --> 00:20:29,770
The time has finally come.

589
00:20:29,830 --> 00:20:31,670
Today's stars of the Special Recipe...

590
00:20:32,700 --> 00:20:33,840
That's us.

591
00:20:34,370 --> 00:20:35,370
It's Tomoya and Yu.

592
00:20:36,770 --> 00:20:39,239
(Top-notch cooking skills come standard.)

593
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:41,910
(Yu, the multi-talented guy who even conquered baking)

594
00:20:41,980 --> 00:20:43,580
Did you take cooking lessons, or?

595
00:20:43,650 --> 00:20:47,050
No, I never took lessons. I just helped my mom out.

596
00:20:47,120 --> 00:20:48,750
- So naturally. - Exactly.

597
00:20:48,820 --> 00:20:50,260
I have a feeling it'll be delicious.

598
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:51,760
We tried it, and it was really good.

599
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:52,890
It was so good.

600
00:20:53,760 --> 00:20:54,990
I can't wait to eat it.

601
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:57,136
(Even talented at cooking. What is NEXZ's special meal?)

602
00:20:57,160 --> 00:20:58,430
Tear it up.

603
00:20:58,960 --> 00:21:00,530
I will use my wok.

604
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:01,969
(Impressive wrist work and great form)

605
00:21:01,970 --> 00:21:03,669
(Their cutting skill is just like professionals.)

606
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:05,216
(The kitchen is overwhelmed by their vibe.)

607
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:06,570
All right, please bring it out.

608
00:21:08,440 --> 00:21:09,770
The broth is so good.

609
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:11,170
Good, right? It is so good.

610
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:12,480
Number 1 among all 4 episodes.

611
00:21:12,580 --> 00:21:14,040
Minnie might feel a bit hurt.

612
00:21:14,180 --> 00:21:15,579
(But he has already changed his mind.)

613
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:18,049
Yu, you should just become a chef.

614
00:21:18,050 --> 00:21:19,050
(NEXZ's recipe that captured...)

615
00:21:19,050 --> 00:21:20,050
(Kim Poong's heart is revealed in a moment.)

616
00:21:20,051 --> 00:21:23,950
All right, let's see...

617
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:25,420
how amazingly Tomoya and Yu cooked.

618
00:21:25,690 --> 00:21:26,890
Let's watch their video.

619
00:21:26,960 --> 00:21:28,706
- I can't wait. - I'm looking forward to it.

620
00:21:28,730 --> 00:21:30,690
Let's go. I'm so excited.

621
00:21:31,160 --> 00:21:32,160
All set.

622
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:34,260
- One, two, three. Hello. - Hello.

623
00:21:34,330 --> 00:21:36,469
We're Next Generation NEXZ's Tomoya.

624
00:21:36,470 --> 00:21:37,470
And I'm Yu.

625
00:21:38,870 --> 00:21:39,940
Thank you.

626
00:21:40,270 --> 00:21:41,270
Hello.

627
00:21:41,770 --> 00:21:42,770
Someone else is here.

628
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:44,640
Hello, I'm Seita.

629
00:21:44,710 --> 00:21:45,710
Just show your face.

630
00:21:46,010 --> 00:21:47,010
Seita?

631
00:21:48,350 --> 00:21:50,750
- How cute. - Where are we?

632
00:21:50,810 --> 00:21:53,449
This is our place, our dorm.

633
00:21:53,450 --> 00:21:55,350
Actually, Yu is in charge of cooking...

634
00:21:55,420 --> 00:21:56,566
- Right, right. - at the dorm.

635
00:21:56,590 --> 00:21:59,790
(Yu's dorm cooking collection)

636
00:21:59,860 --> 00:22:03,629
So basically, Yu will cook, and I will...

637
00:22:03,630 --> 00:22:05,200
(Reading his face)

638
00:22:05,430 --> 00:22:07,236
- Aren't you in charge of eating? - That's right.

639
00:22:07,260 --> 00:22:08,370
Eating is what I do best.

640
00:22:08,430 --> 00:22:09,790
- Exactly. - The designated eater.

641
00:22:09,830 --> 00:22:12,939
- He eats really well. - So all you have to do is cook.

642
00:22:12,940 --> 00:22:14,780
- Almost... - For eating, just leave it to me.

643
00:22:14,870 --> 00:22:16,316
- Please help me out. - Yu might be cooking everything.

644
00:22:16,340 --> 00:22:18,116
- I think that's how it should be. - All right.

645
00:22:18,140 --> 00:22:21,280
- Yes. - The theme is travel. Travel.

646
00:22:21,610 --> 00:22:22,610
Travel.

647
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:24,380
When you think of travel,

648
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:27,579
- what comes to mind? - Well, we went to Osaka,

649
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:29,949
- and even Jeju Island. - We also went to Busan...

650
00:22:29,950 --> 00:22:30,990
We went to Busan too.

651
00:22:32,020 --> 00:22:33,020
Do you know this?

652
00:22:33,190 --> 00:22:36,329
This is Fukuoka's mizutaki from Japan.

653
00:22:36,330 --> 00:22:37,529
Right. Mizutaki is delicious.

654
00:22:37,530 --> 00:22:39,659
It's kind of a Fukuoka version of Korean whole chicken soup.

655
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:41,260
- You're right. - This looks amazing.

656
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:44,970
This is the seafood ramyeon we had on Jeju Island.

657
00:22:45,970 --> 00:22:47,439
This was so, so good.

658
00:22:47,440 --> 00:22:49,080
The seafood ramyeon was truly incredible.

659
00:22:51,140 --> 00:22:53,880
So many dishes came up, right?

660
00:22:54,180 --> 00:22:56,580
That is how serious we are about food.

661
00:22:57,250 --> 00:22:58,649
(Showing off their photo gallery filled with food)

662
00:22:58,650 --> 00:22:59,980
There are so many.

663
00:23:00,980 --> 00:23:02,420
That sweet-and-sour pork looks good.

664
00:23:02,620 --> 00:23:03,620
What would be good?

665
00:23:05,590 --> 00:23:07,550
I think we need to combine it with Japanese food.

666
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:12,036
When you are too good, it is hard to decide.

667
00:23:12,060 --> 00:23:13,836
If you are good at so many things, you wonder what to...

668
00:23:13,860 --> 00:23:14,940
What are you going to make?

669
00:23:16,570 --> 00:23:18,046
Even they are discussing in a flight?

670
00:23:18,070 --> 00:23:19,170
What should I cook?

671
00:23:20,070 --> 00:23:21,740
It is tough. What else is there?

672
00:23:21,810 --> 00:23:23,216
- South America too? - South America too?

673
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:24,810
What will they cook?

674
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:29,050
We didn't mean to put this much pressure on you.

675
00:23:29,350 --> 00:23:30,710
I think we thought about it a lot.

676
00:23:30,780 --> 00:23:32,180
For over a week.

677
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:33,456
You agonized over it for over a week?

678
00:23:33,480 --> 00:23:34,480
Really?

679
00:23:35,590 --> 00:23:36,590
They are revealing it.

680
00:23:37,790 --> 00:23:38,990
The day has finally arrived.

681
00:23:40,990 --> 00:23:43,530
Today is the day we make the menu...

682
00:23:43,590 --> 00:23:45,530
that we spent so much time deliberating on.

683
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:46,860
That is right. Exactly.

684
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:49,700
I am feeling a little nervous. How about you?

685
00:23:49,770 --> 00:23:51,200
- I am excited. - Really?

686
00:23:51,270 --> 00:23:53,440
Yes, I am actually just here to help,

687
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:54,870
so it is all up to Yu.

688
00:23:55,070 --> 00:23:57,710
As for me, I am trembling.

689
00:23:57,770 --> 00:23:59,210
I am just so nervous.

690
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:02,480
All right, Mama Yu, what is today's menu?

691
00:24:02,980 --> 00:24:04,109
Yes, for today's menu...

692
00:24:04,110 --> 00:24:06,750
When Koreans visit Japan, they always eat ramen, right?

693
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:08,390
Oh, you're making ramen?

694
00:24:08,450 --> 00:24:09,666
(A must-eat item when traveling in Japan)

695
00:24:09,690 --> 00:24:12,260
On the other hand, when Japanese people visit Korea,

696
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:13,889
- they eat whole chicken soup. - They eat whole chicken soup.

697
00:24:13,890 --> 00:24:15,360
Of course. It's a classic.

698
00:24:16,260 --> 00:24:19,060
So we combined them to make Kkoko Ramen.

699
00:24:19,460 --> 00:24:20,560
That is a cute name.

700
00:24:21,230 --> 00:24:22,276
Kkoko Ramen sounds delicious.

701
00:24:22,300 --> 00:24:23,830
Kkoko Ramen. That is a great name.

702
00:24:23,900 --> 00:24:25,540
- Love it, love it. - Love it, love it.

703
00:24:25,640 --> 00:24:26,640
And one more thing.

704
00:24:27,070 --> 00:24:28,339
Usually, when people visit Japan,

705
00:24:28,340 --> 00:24:31,440
- we recommend onigiri. - That is true.

706
00:24:32,740 --> 00:24:34,256
- It looks delicious. - Sounds amazing.

707
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:35,556
Those two will go so well together.

708
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:38,380
So we thought of the Korean equivalent, gimbap.

709
00:24:38,580 --> 00:24:39,580
Yes.

710
00:24:39,581 --> 00:24:41,589
(If there's onigiri in Japan, there's gimbap in Korea.)

711
00:24:41,590 --> 00:24:42,590
Nice, nice.

712
00:24:43,290 --> 00:24:45,390
When you think of ramen, it has to be gimbap.

713
00:24:45,490 --> 00:24:46,820
- Exactly. - The perfect pair.

714
00:24:46,990 --> 00:24:50,360
- Ramen and gimbap are inseparable. - They really are inseparable.

715
00:24:50,590 --> 00:24:52,829
However, making just a regular gimbap...

716
00:24:52,830 --> 00:24:53,860
would be a bit boring.

717
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:55,506
Yes, it is not just any ordinary recipe.

718
00:24:55,530 --> 00:24:58,200
Right. There is a Japanese dish...

719
00:24:58,270 --> 00:24:59,940
that I personally love, called shogayaki.

720
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:01,069
It is a dish made with ginger...

721
00:25:01,070 --> 00:25:02,970
- Shogayaki? - It uses ginger.

722
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:05,080
- Sounds delicious. - That sauce looks incredible.

723
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:08,379
So what if we combine the two?

724
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:10,279
(If he combines Shogayaki and gimbap, what will be created?)

725
00:25:10,280 --> 00:25:12,040
- That looks so good. - Sounds great, right?

726
00:25:12,420 --> 00:25:16,589
(It's not a paused screen.)

727
00:25:16,590 --> 00:25:19,090
The name of this gimbap is Buta Gimbap.

728
00:25:19,160 --> 00:25:20,890
It is pork gimbap, hence Buta Gimbap.

729
00:25:21,990 --> 00:25:23,660
- Kkoko Ramen. - And Buta Gimbap.

730
00:25:24,260 --> 00:25:25,500
Let's crush it.

731
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:26,560
Let's go.

732
00:25:27,430 --> 00:25:29,146
I think the quality is going to be top-notch today.

733
00:25:29,170 --> 00:25:32,270
- This is so great. - I can already tell it's delicious.

734
00:25:33,840 --> 00:25:35,470
- Shall we get started? - Sure.

735
00:25:35,570 --> 00:25:36,570
Chef Yu,

736
00:25:36,670 --> 00:25:38,909
- what should we do first? - Time for this.

737
00:25:38,910 --> 00:25:40,980
(Time for this.)

738
00:25:41,280 --> 00:25:43,680
I'll be using my own wok. Yes.

739
00:25:44,010 --> 00:25:46,580
- Shall I bring the knives? - Wow, I should take this knife too.

740
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:47,960
You brought your personal knife?

741
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:50,250
- This one's the most expensive. - How much was it?

742
00:25:50,320 --> 00:25:51,880
- Two hundred... - Two hundred dollars?

743
00:25:51,920 --> 00:25:53,820
- Two hundred dollars. - What? This knife?

744
00:25:54,690 --> 00:25:55,690
Shall we take this?

745
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:57,860
- Oh, nice. - Here's the black pepper.

746
00:25:58,030 --> 00:25:59,030
Okay.

747
00:25:59,160 --> 00:26:00,960
- This is vinegar. - Yes.

748
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:03,080
This soy sauce is from my family back home.

749
00:26:03,130 --> 00:26:05,440
- How precious. - Yes, this is mirin.

750
00:26:05,500 --> 00:26:07,376
- He is so serious about this. - Totally serious.

751
00:26:07,400 --> 00:26:10,640
Because bringing your own equipment to cook...

752
00:26:10,710 --> 00:26:12,070
means you are so serious about it.

753
00:26:12,780 --> 00:26:13,780
I can assure you of that.

754
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:19,220
(Favorite tools ready to go)

755
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:21,790
Now, let's start by prepping the ingredients.

756
00:26:21,890 --> 00:26:22,890
Sounds good.

757
00:26:23,190 --> 00:26:25,820
The ingredients seem rather simple.

758
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:27,280
- It doesn't look too hard. - Yes.

759
00:26:27,420 --> 00:26:29,860
While I sear the chicken here,

760
00:26:30,290 --> 00:26:31,760
